    "description": "Financial compliance and control connect banking rules with the records, responsibilities, and checks used to implement them. In Iran, understanding this field requires separating domestic institutional practice, international supervisory standards, and external assessments of financial-crime safeguards.",
    "tags": ["Banking", "Financial controls", "Compliance", "Internal audit", "Iran"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T18:31:36Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T18:31:36Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Financial Compliance and Control in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand financial controls, independent audit, and international assessments relevant to Iran’s banking systems, with explicit evidence limitations."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "control-purpose",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From rules to accountable operations",
        "content": "A banking control framework establishes who may commit funds, authorize payments, record balances, and check that records agree. The Basel Committee on Banking Supervision, an international banking standard setter hosted by the Bank for International Settlements, treats separation of responsibilities, reconciliation, asset protection, and independent control functions as essential supervisory concerns. These are international benchmarks; their publication does not establish implementation by an Iranian bank. [s1](https://www.bis.org/committees/bcbs/basel-framework/standard/bcp/40/inforce/2024-04-25/published/2024-04-25)\n\nCompliance and internal audit have distinct purposes. Compliance helps management address applicable obligations. Internal audit independently examines whether governance, controls, and compliance processes work. A bank can therefore have documented procedures while still requiring evidence that staff follow them and that weaknesses receive corrective action. [s1](https://www.bis.org/committees/bcbs/basel-framework/standard/bcp/40/inforce/2024-04-25/published/2024-04-25)",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "iran-assessment",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Iran’s international assessment",
        "content": "The Financial Action Task Force, or FATF, sets international standards against money laundering, terrorist financing, and proliferation financing. Its June 2026 statement maintained its call for countermeasures concerning Iran. FATF acknowledged renewed engagement and an update concerning treaty ratification, while judging Iran’s action plan incomplete and its treaty reservations overly broad. This is FATF’s assessment, rather than an independent examination of every Iranian bank. [s2](https://www.fatf-gafi.org/en/publications/High-risk-and-other-monitored-jurisdictions/call-for-action-june-2026.html)\n\nThe statement connects institutional commitments with implementation: ratification alone did not satisfy FATF’s assessment. For readers examining banking technology, this illustrates why a legal announcement, a software installation, and demonstrated compliance are different kinds of evidence. The statement does not provide institution-level measurements of control effectiveness. [s2](https://www.fatf-gafi.org/en/publications/High-risk-and-other-monitored-jurisdictions/call-for-action-june-2026.html)",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "audit-accountability",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What independent assurance establishes",
        "content": "Basel guidance assigns management responsibility for implementing controls and gives internal audit an independent assurance role. Audit needs access to records and the ability to communicate findings to the board or its audit committee. Its remit includes outsourced activities, so transferring technical work to a supplier does not remove that work from the scope of assurance. [s3](https://www.bis.org/committees/bcbs/basel-consolidated-guidelines/module/iac/10)\n\nThe guidance also separates finding a weakness from resolving it. Management must take corrective action, while audit follows up and reports unresolved findings. This provides a useful framework for interpreting claims about financial-control products: a monitoring tool can supply evidence, but effective control also depends on responsibility, review, and remediation. Whether particular Iranian institutions meet these expectations remains outside the evidence verified for this page. [s3](https://www.bis.org/committees/bcbs/basel-consolidated-guidelines/module/iac/10)",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "assurance-relationships",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "An international benchmark for control accountability",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  B[\"Board or audit committee\"] -->|\"Oversees\"| A[\"Independent internal audit\"]\n  M[\"Senior management\"] -->|\"Implements and maintains\"| C[\"Internal controls\"]\n  A -->|\"Independently assesses\"| C\n  A -->|\"Reports findings\"| B\n  A -->|\"Communicates weaknesses\"| M\n  M -->|\"Takes corrective action\"| R[\"Remediation\"]\n```",
        "summary": "The arrows distinguish oversight, implementation, assessment, reporting, and corrective action. This depicts Basel guidance, not a verified organizational chart for an Iranian institution.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      }
    ],
